            <ArticleTitle>Long-term management of liver transplant recipients: A review for the internist.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>361-72</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.3949/ccjm.82a.14072</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Outcomes and long-term survival rates after liver transplantation have significantly improved over the last 5 decades, but the improved longevity is accompanied by long-term complications such as metabolic syndrome, obesity, cardiovascular disease, cancer, and bone disease. After the first year, the primary care physician assumes a greater role and provides most of the patient's care. This review provides a source for primary care physicians in managing the long-term medical complications seen in these patients.</AbstractText>
